WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.931
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4404.

2
00:00:00.931 --> 00:00:01.862
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.862 --> 00:00:06.207
<v JENNIFER ABRAHAM CRAMER>Hi, today is January 23, in the year 2015, and I'm Jennifer Abraham Cramer.

4
00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:10.862
I'm representing the LSU [Louisiana State University] Library’s T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History,

5
00:00:10.862 --> 00:00:17.379
and we are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

6
00:00:17.379 --> 00:00:20.793
To kick that off, we are holding Mossville Oral History Day,

7
00:00:20.793 --> 00:00:24.828
here at the Rigmaiden Center . . . the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:24.828 --> 00:00:28.241
So, today . . . This is the very first interview of the day!

9
00:00:28.241 --> 00:00:32.276
And I am here with two people: Dorothy Felix and Evelyn Gasaway Shelton.

10
00:00:32.276 --> 00:00:35.379
So, thank you so much for agreeing to be interviewed.

11
00:00:35.379 --> 00:00:38.793
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen minutes,

12
00:00:38.793 --> 00:00:45.310
and we will likely touch on . . . Be in touch with you later on to set up a longer, more extensive interview.

13
00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.414
Kind of . . . we're doing a little bit of fact-finding first.

14
00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:55.552
So, if you are interested in continuing on with this, please let us know, and we definitely will be in touch with you.

15
00:00:55.552 --> 00:00:58.034
We'll probably be in touch with you anyway.

16
00:00:58.034 --> 00:01:03.000
Alright, so, can each of you please state your full names, starting with you, Dorothy?

17
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.732
<v DOROTHY FELIX>Dorothy Hartman Felix.

18
00:01:04.732 --> 00:01:05.310
<v CRAMER>Okay.

19
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.042
<v EVELYN GASAWAY SHELTON>Evelyn Gasaway Shelton.

20
00:01:07.042 --> 00:01:07.620
<v CRAMER>Alright.

21
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:12.817
So Ms. Dorothy, when and where were you born?

22
00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:19.746
<v FELIX>Back then, they called it anything . . . Westlake, Louisiana. [Felix and Shelton laugh]

23
00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:23.211
<v SHELTON>[Oh god, you're bad?] [all laugh]

24
00:01:23.211 --> 00:01:28.986
<v FELIX>It may have been called Bayou Den at that time.

25
00:01:28.986 --> 00:01:32.451
It was not directly in Mossville.

26
00:01:32.451 --> 00:01:41.690
It's a little section about a mile away from here . . . That was known as Bayou Den.

27
00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:44.000
<v CRAMER>And that's D-E-N?

28
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.833
<v FELIX>B-A-Y-O-U . . . Apostrophe

29
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:46.750
<v SHELTON AND FELIX>[indistinguishable]

30
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:47.667
<v BILL SHEARMAN>D-apostrophe-I-N-D-E

31
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.417
<v CRAMER>Thank you, Bill!

32
00:01:50.417 --> 00:02:01.417
And that's Bill Shearman, by the way, everybody. [Felix and Shelton laugh]

33
00:02:01.417 --> 00:02:06.000
<v FELIX>Bill Shearman. [laughs] Okay!

34
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.798
<v CRAMER>So Ms. Evelyn, when and where were you born?

35
00:02:10.798 --> 00:02:13.463
<v SHELTON>Wiergate, Texas . . . In east Texas.

36
00:02:13.463 --> 00:02:13.996
<v CRAMER>When?

37
00:02:13.996 --> 00:02:15.062
<v SHELTON>Beg pardon?

38
00:02:15.062 --> 00:02:16.661
<v CRAMER>In what year?

39
00:02:16.661 --> 00:02:18.261
<v SHELTON>September 24, 1940.

40
00:02:18.261 --> 00:02:18.794
<v CRAMER>Okay.

41
00:02:18.794 --> 00:02:26.257
And did you [speaking to Mrs. Felix] mention your year, when you were born?

42
00:02:26.257 --> 00:02:27.323
<v FELIX>Nobody's business.

43
00:02:27.323 --> 00:02:36.385
<v CRAMER>[laughs] Okay, but I know it! [all laugh] Okay, and, Mrs. Dorothy what were your parents' names?

44
00:02:36.385 --> 00:02:39.584
<v FELIX>Earl Hartman and [Loraine?] Fischer Hartman.

45
00:02:39.584 --> 00:02:41.183
She remarried, Cole.

46
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:41.716
<v CRAMER>Okay.

47
00:02:41.716 --> 00:02:43.848
And what about you?

48
00:02:43.848 --> 00:02:47.047
<v SHELTON>Mine. . . [Casey?] Gasaway and Frankie Gasaway.

49
00:02:47.047 --> 00:02:47.580
<v CRAMER>Okay.

50
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:54.510
And, each of you . . . Where were you families . . . Where is your family from?

51
00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.175
What brought them to Mossville?

52
00:02:57.175 --> 00:03:02.506
<v FELIX>My family . . . Or both parents were from this area also.

53
00:03:02.506 --> 00:03:12.101
My mom was from a little section called Lockport [Louisiana] which is just . . . Not too far from here.

54
00:03:12.101 --> 00:03:20.097
My father is from this also. . . From a little further down, maybe a mile away.

55
00:03:20.097 --> 00:03:24.895
This was his home on the highway, Highway 90.

56
00:03:24.895 --> 00:03:29.693
His parents moved there . . . I don't remember the year,

57
00:03:29.693 --> 00:03:38.222
but that's the only place I've known him to be, is up there on the highway.

58
00:03:38.222 --> 00:03:40.887
<v CRAMER>What are your grandparents' names?

59
00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:44.086
We'll start with your dad's parents.

60
00:03:44.086 --> 00:03:48.883
<v FELIX>My dad's parents was Allie Hartman and Tom Hartman.

61
00:03:48.883 --> 00:03:55.813
My mom's parents was Irene Lee Fischer, and my grandfather was Frank Fischer.

62
00:03:55.813 --> 00:04:00.611
I can even go back to my great - grandparents.

63
00:04:00.611 --> 00:04:01.677
<v CRAMER>Please do.

64
00:04:01.677 --> 00:04:05.942
<v FELIX>My dad's was Tom Hartman and Cora Hartman.

65
00:04:05.942 --> 00:04:08.607
That was my dad's grandparents.

66
00:04:08.607 --> 00:04:13.405
My mom's grandparents was [Lonise?] Lee, Lonise [Cotlong?] Lee.

67
00:04:13.405 --> 00:04:16.603
Then her husband was Jim Lee.

68
00:04:16.603 --> 00:04:18.735
So that's my great.

69
00:04:18.735 --> 00:04:19.268
Yes.

70
00:04:19.268 --> 00:04:23.000
So, I can witness seven generations.

71
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.458
<v CRAMER>Now where were your grandparents from?

72
00:04:26.458 --> 00:04:29.339
<v FELIX>They were from this area.

73
00:04:29.339 --> 00:04:33.949
All of my grandparents were from this area.

74
00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:43.746
I'm not quite sure about . . . I said all of them . . . I'm not quite sure about that great-grandfather.

75
00:04:43.746 --> 00:04:48.356
I'm not . . . He's not from this immediate area.

76
00:04:48.356 --> 00:04:53.542
He's from somewhere away, and I'm not sure where.

77
00:04:53.542 --> 00:04:54.119
<v CRAMER>Okay.

78
00:04:54.119 --> 00:04:57.000
What about you, Mrs. Evelyn?

79
00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.610
Where were your . . . Tell me about your parents.

80
00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:11.407
<v SHELTON>My parents . . . My grandparents on my dad's side were Bob Gasaway, and my grandmother was Lily Gasaway.

81
00:05:11.407 --> 00:05:18.322
My grandfather was a barber . . . That's what he did for a living.

82
00:05:18.322 --> 00:05:20.627
On my mom's side,

83
00:05:20.627 --> 00:05:28.695
my grandfather was . . . Getting back to my dad's side . . . They lived in Texas . . . Wiergate,

84
00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:33.881
Texas . . . which is a little town near Newton, Texas.

85
00:05:33.881 --> 00:05:41.373
If you know where Newton or Jasper, Texas . . . Okay, well, in that vicinity.

86
00:05:41.373 --> 00:05:48.864
Okay, and my mom . . . My mom's parents are David Spikes and Della Spikes.

87
00:05:48.864 --> 00:05:51.169
David came from Georgia.

88
00:05:51.169 --> 00:05:59.814
From what I can understand, he was a freed slave when he came to Louisiana.

89
00:05:59.814 --> 00:06:03.847
They sharecropped, did a lot of farming.

90
00:06:03.847 --> 00:06:05.000
<v CRAMER>Yes.

91
00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:11.923
<v SHELTON>They were from Bancroft, Louisiana, which is near . . . between DeQuincy and DeRidder, I believe, okay. [laughs]

92
00:06:11.923 --> 00:06:15.385
<v CRAMER>Do you know why they came to Mossville?

93
00:06:15.385 --> 00:06:15.817
<v SHELTON>Oh . . .

94
00:06:15.817 --> 00:06:18.846
<v CRAMER>Or why they came to this area?

95
00:06:18.846 --> 00:06:21.010
<v SHELTON>I know why my mom . . .

96
00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:21.442
<v CRAMER>Sure.

97
00:06:21.442 --> 00:06:22.308
<v SHELTON>And Dad . . .

98
00:06:22.308 --> 00:06:23.173
<v CRAMER>Yes, please.

99
00:06:23.173 --> 00:06:27.933
<v SHELTON>Because they were looking for a better life for their family.

100
00:06:27.933 --> 00:06:32.260
And at that time, after the war, after the Depression,

101
00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:40.913
many of the families were moving to different sections of the country for better life . . . So, they came to Louisiana.

102
00:06:40.913 --> 00:06:43.942
They heard, you know, about the industries.

103
00:06:43.942 --> 00:06:45.673
That was in 1943.

104
00:06:45.673 --> 00:06:50.000
<v CRAMER>So what kind of work did your dad do?

105
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.622
<v SHELTON>He worked at Cities Service [oil and gas company, now Citgo].

106
00:06:54.622 --> 00:06:57.143
He started off as a laborer.

107
00:06:57.143 --> 00:07:00.504
When he retired, he was a truck driver.

108
00:07:00.504 --> 00:07:03.025
He did truck-driving for many years.

109
00:07:03.025 --> 00:07:06.387
<v CRAMER>And did your mom work outside the home?

110
00:07:06.387 --> 00:07:06.807
<v SHELTON>Yes.

111
00:07:06.807 --> 00:07:09.748
Not until we got up in age.

112
00:07:09.748 --> 00:07:14.370
I mean, she started school after most of us started school.

113
00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:19.412
Then she worked outside . . . She worked at the school as a cook.

114
00:07:19.412 --> 00:07:24.874
But she stayed home with us until most of us got into school.

115
00:07:24.874 --> 00:07:26.555
<v FELIX>She was our seamstress.

116
00:07:26.555 --> 00:07:27.395
<v SHELTON>Oh yeah!

117
00:07:27.395 --> 00:07:29.916
She did a lot of sewing.

118
00:07:29.916 --> 00:07:32.437
<v FELIX>She did a lot of sewing!

119
00:07:32.437 --> 00:07:36.639
<v SHELTON>Yeah, we stayed in pretty little dresses, frilly dresses. [laughs]

120
00:07:36.639 --> 00:07:40.000
<v CRAMER>And what did your parents do, Dorothy?

121
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.523
<v FELIX>My father worked for a company called [Sauter?] as a truck-driver. . . Lumber company.

122
00:07:45.523 --> 00:07:48.497
He worked for them so many years.

123
00:07:48.497 --> 00:07:50.196
That was my father.

124
00:07:50.196 --> 00:07:55.719
Now, my mom was at home during the time we were growing up.

125
00:07:55.719 --> 00:07:57.843
Then she started working out.

126
00:07:57.843 --> 00:07:59.118
My grandfather was

127
00:07:59.118 --> 00:08:05.915
<c.p>a well-digger, a horse-tamer. [laughs] He also worked at the plant called Kellogg at that time.

128
00:08:05.915 --> 00:08:11.438
It's now called . . . Gosh, what’s that plant named y'all, when you go down . . .

129
00:08:11.438 --> 00:08:13.137
<v SHELTON>My memory is bad.

130
00:08:13.137 --> 00:08:19.510
<v FELIX>Mine is bad, but I can remember it was Kellogg . . . I remember it was Kellogg.

131
00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:21.634
I remember it was now.

132
00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:22.908
<v SHELTON>I remember Kellogg.

133
00:08:22.908 --> 00:08:25.458
<v FELIX>Yeah, that's where he was, Kellogg.

134
00:08:25.458 --> 00:08:27.582
<v SHELTON>I remember that name now.

135
00:08:27.582 --> 00:08:32.255
<v FELIX>But I forgot who took the place . . . Is right down there.

136
00:08:32.255 --> 00:08:33.954
<v CRAMER>What did they do?

137
00:08:33.954 --> 00:08:36.078
What was the plant manufacturing?

138
00:08:36.078 --> 00:08:38.627
<v FELIX>What was Kellogg, at that time?

139
00:08:38.627 --> 00:08:42.026
At that time . . . That's back in the 40s.

140
00:08:42.026 --> 00:08:42.876
<v SHELTON>That's 40s.

141
00:08:42.876 --> 00:08:45.000
We were little girls.

142
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.751
<v FELIX>Probably chemicals, now that we're dying of.

143
00:08:47.751 --> 00:08:50.896
Just like then. [laughs] And it carried on.

144
00:08:50.896 --> 00:08:51.289
<v SHELTON>Yes.

145
00:08:51.289 --> 00:08:54.826
<v FELIX>Yes, but that was the name of it, Kellogg.

146
00:08:54.826 --> 00:08:56.005
K-E-L-L-O-G-G, I think.

147
00:08:56.005 --> 00:08:59.935
<v CRAMER>What kind of schooling did each of your parents have?

148
00:08:59.935 --> 00:09:03.473
<v FELIX>My mom went as far as the seventh grade.

149
00:09:03.473 --> 00:09:07.403
They all went up to that age, the seventh grade.

150
00:09:07.403 --> 00:09:10.547
With the understanding and the knowledge they had,

151
00:09:10.547 --> 00:09:18.408
they have more common sense and knowledge and wisdom than these kids that are getting college degrees and everything now.

152
00:09:18.408 --> 00:09:23.910
Because, if they had not had the understanding and the knowledge that they had,

153
00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:29.413
I don't believe they would have survived and done as well as they did.

154
00:09:29.413 --> 00:09:31.378
Because, they were smart people.

155
00:09:31.378 --> 00:09:38.453
They may not . . . Some of them could not write, read and write, but they had it up here.

156
00:09:38.453 --> 00:09:40.418
You could not out-think them.

157
00:09:40.418 --> 00:09:43.955
They had the wits, and they were . . . You know,

158
00:09:43.955 --> 00:09:51.423
I was blessed to just grow up and see how well they could survive and do things and understand.

159
00:09:51.423 --> 00:09:53.388
It’s just . . . It's just amazing.

160
00:09:53.388 --> 00:09:56.532
To see some of what's going on nowadays.

161
00:09:56.532 --> 00:09:58.498
Some of those children’s minds.

162
00:09:58.498 --> 00:10:02.821
They have technology, but as far as that wisdom up there . . .

163
00:10:02.821 --> 00:10:04.000
<v SHELTON>The reason.

164
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.954
<v CRAMER>And one of the things I'm doing to do, too,

165
00:10:06.954 --> 00:10:12.566
when we come back for the next interviews . . . I'm going to come back and dig around a little bit,

166
00:10:12.566 --> 00:10:17.587
even deeper, to find out more about your parents and your grandparents and that sort of thing.

167
00:10:17.587 --> 00:10:23.790
One thing I'd like to say for the listeners, too . . . Since we have two people, I'm going to extend the session,

168
00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:29.993
because we don't have anybody signed up for this 1:30 session . . . We don't have somebody signed up for the 1:30 session.

169
00:10:29.993 --> 00:10:32.060
So, I don't want to rush this.

170
00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:36.491
I think this is a good interview, and I want to continue on with it.

171
00:10:36.491 --> 00:10:40.331
So I'm going to continue on with it for a little bit longer.

172
00:10:40.331 --> 00:10:43.875
<v FELIX>I did mention . . . I didn't mention, too . . . The reason for being here . . .

173
00:10:43.875 --> 00:10:44.171
<v CRAMER>Yes.

174
00:10:44.171 --> 00:10:49.783
<v FELIX>Is that we were in that section over there, that PPG [a chemical and material manufacturer] is right now,

175
00:10:49.783 --> 00:10:55.986
and we saw no hope there . . . That is was not going to develop, that it was not going to do anything.

176
00:10:55.986 --> 00:11:00.121
So, I won't go into the big story . . . Then we moved to this section.

177
00:11:00.121 --> 00:11:03.075
This is the section that was founded during the 1700s.

178
00:11:03.075 --> 00:11:05.438
I hear some people say in the 1800s.

179
00:11:05.438 --> 00:11:07.505
This was found by an African-American man.

180
00:11:07.505 --> 00:11:12.231
The reason is because that was during a time where racial problems were all . . . Very strong.

181
00:11:12.231 --> 00:11:21.388
Most of the people that came here were looking for a safe haven . . . Somewhere that they could be away from all the hostility and all the cruelty that was going on.

182
00:11:21.388 --> 00:11:23.456
So, one by one, families moved in.

183
00:11:23.456 --> 00:11:26.114
When we moved in here, it was mostly woods . . .

184
00:11:26.114 --> 00:11:27.000
<v SHELTON>Yes, [rural?].

185
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.163
<v FELIX>Just primitive-style living.

186
00:11:28.163 --> 00:11:31.652
So, we were able to come here, live here,

187
00:11:31.652 --> 00:11:40.181
and see the town develop into a very good neighborhood where we saw homes develop . . . We saw stores, we saw little cafes.

188
00:11:40.181 --> 00:11:42.507
A lot of things changed, too.

189
00:11:42.507 --> 00:11:49.097
That was during the time that . . . When it grew, we had a very good place to live.

190
00:11:49.097 --> 00:11:55.300
We did not have to worry about closing the doors at night and running from this.

191
00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:57.626
It was just a healthy environment.

192
00:11:57.626 --> 00:12:04.991
It was clean . . . We did all the gardening and the fishing . . . Everything that you needed, you had it here.

193
00:12:04.991 --> 00:12:08.480
This was a very healthy environment to live in.

194
00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:10.031
That's why we're here.

195
00:12:10.031 --> 00:12:12.357
<v CRAMER>So where exactly did you live?

196
00:12:12.357 --> 00:12:18.947
<v FELIX>I live where PPG is housed . . . PPG plant is over near the freeway, whatever . . . Pete Manena road.

197
00:12:18.947 --> 00:12:22.824
We were in a little section there where we lived.

198
00:12:22.824 --> 00:12:25.150
<v CRAMER>Did it have a neighborhood name?

199
00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:29.414
<v FELIX>It wasn't but about . . . I think it was seven homes there.

200
00:12:29.414 --> 00:12:30.965
That was family members.

201
00:12:30.965 --> 00:12:34.841
So, when one moved, they all moved to this section.

202
00:12:34.841 --> 00:12:40.656
It was divided in a section, but it did not have a little community name.

203
00:12:40.656 --> 00:12:45.308
We were just glad to be there and glad to get out.

204
00:12:45.308 --> 00:12:50.736
That was where we were. [Shelton laughs] It was seven homes that were there.

205
00:12:50.736 --> 00:12:55.000
When everybody left, they all left at the same time.

206
00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.606
<v CRAMER>And what about you? [directed to Shelton]

207
00:12:58.606 --> 00:13:03.242
<v SHELTON>My mom . . . My daddy went to the eighth grade.

208
00:13:03.242 --> 00:13:05.303
My mom finished school.

209
00:13:05.303 --> 00:13:13.545
She spent one . . . One year in college, at Prairieview E &amp; M. Then they came this way.

210
00:13:13.545 --> 00:13:20.758
But she started having babies, so . . . [laughs] She never did go back to college.

211
00:13:20.758 --> 00:13:28.485
But she thought education . . . Both of them really instilled in us to get an education.

212
00:13:28.485 --> 00:13:36.727
Many times, we would . . . for entertainment, we'd sit on the floor around her . . . on the floor.

213
00:13:36.727 --> 00:13:43.939
She would read from the The Book of Facts and drill us at night.

214
00:13:43.939 --> 00:13:47.030
Daddy would drill us at night.

215
00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:51.152
We had the kerosene lamps at that time.

216
00:13:51.152 --> 00:13:56.818
He'd drill us on math facts and computational skills and whatnot.

217
00:13:56.818 --> 00:13:59.909
It was all fun for us.

218
00:13:59.909 --> 00:14:03.000
<v CRAMER>So where did you live?

219
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.076
<v SHELTON>Where did I live?

220
00:14:05.076 --> 00:14:06.633
On Prater Road.

221
00:14:06.633 --> 00:14:07.671
<v CRAMER>Okay, okay.

222
00:14:07.671 --> 00:14:12.342
<v SHELTON>Well, as a matter of fact, we were neighbors.

223
00:14:12.342 --> 00:14:12.861
<v CRAMER>Okay.

224
00:14:12.861 --> 00:14:14.418
<v SHELTON>We were neighbors.

225
00:14:14.418 --> 00:14:14.937
<v CRAMER>Alright.

226
00:14:14.937 --> 00:14:22.722
<v SHELTON>When we moved . . . First, we started, I think . . . First, we lived on Old Spanish Trail.

227
00:14:22.722 --> 00:14:24.278
Near the track.

228
00:14:24.278 --> 00:14:25.835
Yes, down here.

229
00:14:25.835 --> 00:14:35.177
Then we moved on Prater Road because my parents rented from her grandfather and . . . So, we were neighbors.

230
00:14:35.177 --> 00:14:35.696
<v CRAMER>Alright.

231
00:14:35.696 --> 00:14:39.848
<v SHELTON>We grew up like sisters at that time.

232
00:14:39.848 --> 00:14:44.000
<v CRAMER>So where did y'all go to school?

233
00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.029
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[in unison] Mossville Elementary.

234
00:14:45.029 --> 00:14:45.800
<v CRAMER>Where was that?

235
00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.857
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[in unison] Right here where we're sitting. [laughs]

236
00:14:47.857 --> 00:14:48.629
<v CRAMER>Where was that?

237
00:14:48.629 --> 00:14:50.686
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[in unison] Right here where we're sitting. [laughs]

238
00:14:50.686 --> 00:14:53.000
<v FELIX>And then we graduated from that, down there.

239
00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:59.750
<v CRAMER>So, the Mossville Elementary School was right here where the Rigmaiden Recreation Center is today?

240
00:14:59.750 --> 00:15:00.200
<v SHELTON>Right.

241
00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:03.350
It was a two-room building on stilts.

242
00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:03.800
<v FELIX>Yes.

243
00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:07.850
<v SHELTON>And we would play underneath the building. [Felix laughs]

244
00:15:07.850 --> 00:15:08.300
<v FELIX>Yes.

245
00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:11.450
The room was divided by a partition.

246
00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:16.850
<v SHELTON>For the wintertime, they had the big . . . what do you call them?

247
00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:19.100
Pot-belly stoves, you would say.

248
00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:20.000
<v FELIX>Oh.

249
00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.054
<v SHELTON>They would bring in . . .

250
00:15:22.054 --> 00:15:23.081
<v CRAMER>The wood?

251
00:15:23.081 --> 00:15:26.162
<v SHELTON>. . . the logs . . . to keep us warm.

252
00:15:26.162 --> 00:15:30.784
We'd put on plays, because we had a stage.

253
00:15:30.784 --> 00:15:33.865
The stage was a portable one.

254
00:15:33.865 --> 00:15:44.649
When we were not on the stage, the stage was underneath the building, and that's where we'd go, underneath the building.

255
00:15:44.649 --> 00:15:48.757
Play and eat . . . and share our lunches with

256
00:15:48.757 --> 00:15:49.784
<c.p>each other.

257
00:15:49.784 --> 00:15:53.378
It was really a close-knit, family-knit community.

258
00:15:53.378 --> 00:15:54.919
Everybody knew everybody.

259
00:15:54.919 --> 00:16:00.054
If you did something wrong, the neighbor could chastise you.

260
00:16:00.054 --> 00:16:02.622
<v CRAMER>So who were your teachers?

261
00:16:02.622 --> 00:16:06.216
<v SHELTON>Mrs. [Ezora?] Lyons was the first one.

262
00:16:06.216 --> 00:16:07.757
<v CRAMER>Mrs. Ezora Lyons?

263
00:16:07.757 --> 00:16:09.811
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[in unison] [Ezora?] Lyons.

264
00:16:09.811 --> 00:16:13.405
<v SHELTON>Later on, we had Mrs. Washington. [V.

265
00:16:13.405 --> 00:16:14.432
M.?] Washington.

266
00:16:14.432 --> 00:16:17.514
Vera Mae Washington was her name.

267
00:16:17.514 --> 00:16:20.081
Then we had Mrs. Mitchell.

268
00:16:20.081 --> 00:16:21.622
<v FELIX>Don't forget Mrs. [?].

269
00:16:21.622 --> 00:16:22.649
Mrs. [Salier?].

270
00:16:22.649 --> 00:16:25.216
Do you remember Mrs. [Salier?].

271
00:16:25.216 --> 00:16:27.270
<v SHELTON>[Salier?] Was it [Salier?].

272
00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:28.811
I know Billips.

273
00:16:28.811 --> 00:16:29.838
<v FELIX>Mrs. Billips.

274
00:16:29.838 --> 00:16:33.946
Let's see. . . Mrs. Mitchell, Mrs. Billips, Mrs. Washington.

275
00:16:33.946 --> 00:16:34.459
Then . . .

276
00:16:34.459 --> 00:16:34.973
<v SHELTON>[indistinguishable]

277
00:16:34.973 --> 00:16:40.108
<v FELIX>. . . used to teach and went to the church up there.

278
00:16:40.108 --> 00:16:48.838
Mrs. [Salier?] [?] went to teach at this church up there . . . Who else came along during that time?

279
00:16:48.838 --> 00:16:52.432
Then we started moving into Mrs. Kilgore.

280
00:16:52.432 --> 00:16:55.000
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[discussing quietly amongst themselves]

281
00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:58.243
<v SHELTON>We had only the two-room building.

282
00:16:58.243 --> 00:17:05.811
Later on, they built another building on the side which housed the upper grades.

283
00:17:05.811 --> 00:17:07.432
<v FELIX>The upper grades.

284
00:17:07.432 --> 00:17:10.135
<v SHELTON>The cafeteria . . . the west side.

285
00:17:10.135 --> 00:17:15.000
<v CRAMER>How many students . . . When y'all were coming up?

286
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.514
<v FELIX>See we had about one, two, three, four rows of students.

287
00:17:18.514 --> 00:17:20.431
Maybe about ten in a row?

288
00:17:20.431 --> 00:17:21.069
<v SHELTON>Yeah, ten.

289
00:17:21.069 --> 00:17:23.625
<v FELIX>When we graduate, there was twenty-two of us.

290
00:17:23.625 --> 00:17:23.944
<v SHELTON>Yeah.

291
00:17:23.944 --> 00:17:27.139
<v FELIX>I remember that . . . twenty-two of us in the graduating class.

292
00:17:27.139 --> 00:17:27.458
Yes.

293
00:17:27.458 --> 00:17:32.569
For the two. . . When the one building was here and we had the two rooms . . . Oh,

294
00:17:32.569 --> 00:17:34.806
we must've had about a hundred kids.

295
00:17:34.806 --> 00:17:38.000
<v SHELTON>Oh, yeah we had to have about a hundred!

296
00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:40.250
<v FELIX>A hundred, about a hundred kids.

297
00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:41.000
That's everything . . .

298
00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.500
<v CRAMER>What were you taught?

299
00:17:42.500 --> 00:17:42.875
<v FELIX>Taught?

300
00:17:42.875 --> 00:17:48.125
We were the best there is! [Felix and Shelton speak excitedly about testing, indistinguishable]

301
00:17:48.125 --> 00:17:49.250
<v CRAMER>What is that?

302
00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:56.375
<v FELIX>That used to be a test . . . You go to Baton Rouge and test you on the skills and all.

303
00:17:56.375 --> 00:17:57.875
I was number six!

304
00:17:57.875 --> 00:18:00.125
<v SHELTON>We always ranked with the highest.

305
00:18:00.125 --> 00:18:02.000
<v CRAMER>Like a standardized test?

306
00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:03.595
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[in unison] Oh yes!

307
00:18:03.595 --> 00:18:11.966
<v CRAMER>What did they . . . So tell us a little bit about the school . . . about that school and about some of the teachers.

308
00:18:11.966 --> 00:18:13.162
<v FELIX>Oh, well, okay.

309
00:18:13.162 --> 00:18:15.953
I can tell you that we had [?].

310
00:18:15.953 --> 00:18:20.736
Now, we might not have had all the modern stuff, like typewriters . . .

311
00:18:20.736 --> 00:18:21.135
<v SHELTON>Computers.

312
00:18:21.135 --> 00:18:25.520
<v FELIX>Computers and all that stuff, but we had teachers that cared.

313
00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:29.108
Teachers that wanted to make sure that you understood,

314
00:18:29.108 --> 00:18:35.088
that you learned what they were teaching . . . So that you could prepare yourself for anything.

315
00:18:35.088 --> 00:18:40.270
We used to have little tests on Fridays to compete with each other.

316
00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:48.642
They . . . And if the teachers saw that you were not understanding or getting . . . They would spend time with you after school,

317
00:18:48.642 --> 00:18:57.412
go to your house . . . or whatever it took to make sure that you were getting what you should get out of school.

318
00:18:57.412 --> 00:18:58.608
They did it.

319
00:18:58.608 --> 00:19:00.203
We had language, arithmetic . . .

320
00:19:00.203 --> 00:19:01.000
<v SHELTON>Math.

321
00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:02.250
<v FELIX>Math, geography, history.

322
00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.333
I mean . . . You learned that.

323
00:19:04.333 --> 00:19:13.083
You might forget it now . . . If you asked me a question about it when I was growing up, I knew it.

324
00:19:13.083 --> 00:19:14.333
It was there.

325
00:19:14.333 --> 00:19:16.833
They made sure you got it.

326
00:19:16.833 --> 00:19:18.083
<v SHELTON>Those multiplication facts!

327
00:19:18.083 --> 00:19:20.167
You knew all of those.

328
00:19:20.167 --> 00:19:21.417
<v FELIX>Oh yes, everything.

329
00:19:21.417 --> 00:19:22.667
You were taught.

330
00:19:22.667 --> 00:19:27.667
I don't care what it was, what it took. . . You were taught.

331
00:19:27.667 --> 00:19:32.250
<v SHELTON>Our books were not usable and such, but we used them.

332
00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:38.083
They were given to us after they had been used by the other schools.

333
00:19:38.083 --> 00:19:38.917
That's fact.

334
00:19:38.917 --> 00:19:41.833
<v CRAMER>By other schools, what do you mean?

335
00:19:41.833 --> 00:19:43.083
The white schools?

336
00:19:43.083 --> 00:19:43.500
<v SHELTON>Yes.

337
00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:44.750
That's a fact.

338
00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:46.000
That happened.

339
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.792
<v FELIX>Hand-me-down books, that's what they were.

340
00:19:48.792 --> 00:19:49.257
But . . .

341
00:19:49.257 --> 00:19:50.653
<v SHELTON>Tattered and torn.

342
00:19:50.653 --> 00:19:53.911
But we were happy to get them.

343
00:19:53.911 --> 00:19:56.238
<v FELIX>But we had the teachers.

344
00:19:56.238 --> 00:20:04.614
I would have put our teachers up against any teacher . . . because they made sure you got that work.

345
00:20:04.614 --> 00:20:11.594
<v SHELTON>Another thing. . . They saw to it that we left from just right here in Mossville.

346
00:20:11.594 --> 00:20:17.178
They would take us on trips to see the capitol of Louisiana.

347
00:20:17.178 --> 00:20:23.693
They would take us to New Orleans to see St. Louis Cathedral, the Cabildo.

348
00:20:23.693 --> 00:20:25.554
Several . . . The Tabasco factory.

349
00:20:25.554 --> 00:20:33.000
The Evangeline oaks where Edgar Allen Poe sat under the tree and wrote the poem . . .

350
00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:34.772
<v FELIX>Things that were interesting.

351
00:20:34.772 --> 00:20:40.532
I mean, they were really, really trips you could get something out of.

352
00:20:40.532 --> 00:20:42.747
We had our glee clubs.

353
00:20:42.747 --> 00:20:49.392
We had our little organizations that we went to, if you were an honor student.

354
00:20:49.392 --> 00:20:49.835
Delta?

355
00:20:49.835 --> 00:20:51.165
<v SHELTON>Delta . . . Sigma Theta.

356
00:20:51.165 --> 00:20:51.608
<v FELIX>Sundays . . .

357
00:20:51.608 --> 00:20:56.924
<v CRAMER>And y'all were classmates? [Felix and Shelton speaking to one another, indistinguishable]

358
00:20:56.924 --> 00:20:57.810
<v FELIX>Yes, yes.

359
00:20:57.810 --> 00:21:00.468
<v CRAMER>And what year did y'all graduate?

360
00:21:00.468 --> 00:21:00.911
<v SHELTON>1958.

361
00:21:00.911 --> 00:21:05.342
<v CRAMER>Okay and so, you started high school in fifty-four, then.

362
00:21:05.342 --> 00:21:06.228
Probably, right?

363
00:21:06.228 --> 00:21:08.000
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[in unison] Yes.

364
00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:11.860
<v SHELTON>We had to go to Washington High for our ninth grade.

365
00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:12.912
<v CRAMER>Where's Washington High?

366
00:21:12.912 --> 00:21:13.614
<v FELIX>Lake Charles.

367
00:21:13.614 --> 00:21:19.930
It's no longer Washington High now, but it was then on Opelousas Street, near Prater and all that.

368
00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:22.035
That's where we had to go.

369
00:21:22.035 --> 00:21:28.351
We were bussed to go there, because we were not allowed to go to the white schools here.

370
00:21:28.351 --> 00:21:31.158
We passed by Westlake. [Felix and Shelton laugh]

371
00:21:31.158 --> 00:21:35.719
<v SHELTON>The kids from Vinton, Sulphur, and Westlake were bussed in to Mossville High.

372
00:21:35.719 --> 00:21:42.737
<v CRAMER>I tell you what . . . When I come back and do interview number two, which I know y'all want do, right?

373
00:21:42.737 --> 00:21:49.404
We're going to go a little more into detail on that . . . I'm kind of going to skip ahead, too.

374
00:21:49.404 --> 00:21:53.965
Because the other things I want to talk to y'all about . . . mentioned gardening.

375
00:21:53.965 --> 00:22:02.035
So I’m kind of going to go back into that at some point . . . Livestock, a description of the house, the creeks, the wells.

376
00:22:02.035 --> 00:22:03.789
You know what I mean?

377
00:22:03.789 --> 00:22:11.860
To really kind of paint a picture of what Mossville was like at the time . . . what this area was like at the time.

378
00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:18.526
So, I'm going to skip ahead a little bit . . . Well, actually, if you . . . If you don't mind indulging me,

379
00:22:18.526 --> 00:22:28.000
I know you were young at the time during World War II . . . But do you have some insight on how Mossville changed during that time period?

380
00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.862
<v FELIX>Well, yeah it changed because it got better!

381
00:22:30.862 --> 00:22:35.512
Like I said, we came out here it was mostly a wooded area.

382
00:22:35.512 --> 00:22:37.658
It wasn't much to go on.

383
00:22:37.658 --> 00:22:39.088
Then, little by little,

384
00:22:39.088 --> 00:22:49.462
they couldn't do everything fast and quick and in a hurry because of the economy . . . But they were able to start building because everybody . . . All families helped each other.

385
00:22:49.462 --> 00:22:51.608
Even when you had maybe a

386
00:22:51.608 --> 00:22:56.615
<c.p>home that didn't have but one bedroom and they wanted to add another bedroom,

387
00:22:56.615 --> 00:23:01.265
then other families would help each other make sure that that was done.

388
00:23:01.265 --> 00:23:03.769
You saw the place begin to grow.

389
00:23:03.769 --> 00:23:09.135
It didn't start growing really until after, you know, you start getting the streets in.

390
00:23:09.135 --> 00:23:15.215
We just got sewage in, not long ago. [Felix and Shelton laugh] So it didn't grow fast,

391
00:23:15.215 --> 00:23:20.938
but we had to deal with the wells . . . We had to deal with no running water.

392
00:23:20.938 --> 00:23:23.442
We had to deal with no lights.

393
00:23:23.442 --> 00:23:29.165
We had all of that to deal with, and we saw all of those things changing.

394
00:23:29.165 --> 00:23:34.173
It meant a lot to see . . . Because, when we first got our lights, woo!

395
00:23:34.173 --> 00:23:37.750
It was a different city because it was lit up.

396
00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:41.327
<v SHELTON>When we first had . . . When the TVs first came in . . .

397
00:23:41.327 --> 00:23:44.904
<v FELIX>Right! [laughs] [Felix and Shelton speaking over one another, indistinguishable]

398
00:23:44.904 --> 00:23:47.050
<v SHELTON>[. . . ?] We'd go there, looking for TVs.

399
00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:48.481
Black and white TVs.

400
00:23:48.481 --> 00:23:49.912
<v FELIX>Black and white TV.

401
00:23:49.912 --> 00:23:55.992
We had a room there with all the kids gathered around . . . would come around and watch TV.

402
00:23:55.992 --> 00:23:59.212
What year was that, when we got the lights?

403
00:23:59.212 --> 00:24:01.000
<v SHELTON>Oh, I don't know.

404
00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:06.861
<v FELIX>I remember [?] going around and asking for people to sign the petition to get the lights.

405
00:24:06.861 --> 00:24:08.693
I mean, [Ham?] Reed was . . .

406
00:24:08.693 --> 00:24:10.158
<v SHELTON>Yeah, was the sheriff.

407
00:24:10.158 --> 00:24:10.891
<v FELIX>Ham Reed.

408
00:24:10.891 --> 00:24:11.624
<v CRAMER>Who's papa?

409
00:24:11.624 --> 00:24:13.089
<v FELIX>Oh, my grandfather. [laughs]

410
00:24:13.089 --> 00:24:13.822
<v CRAMER>Okay, okay.

411
00:24:13.822 --> 00:24:16.752
<v FELIX>I believe Ham Reed was in office then.

412
00:24:16.752 --> 00:24:19.683
It was Ham Reed, [Picard?] was around here.

413
00:24:19.683 --> 00:24:22.980
Brian was the one that started Bel Air . . . Brian.

414
00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:24.812
You know that man, Brian [?]?

415
00:24:24.812 --> 00:24:27.010
<v SHELTON>Oh yeah, yeah I know him.

416
00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:30.673
<v FELIX>He started Bel Air, and we saw Bel Air change.

417
00:24:30.673 --> 00:24:33.970
But the year . . . It had to be the late . . .

418
00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:34.337
<v SHELTON>Fifties?

419
00:24:34.337 --> 00:24:38.000
<v FELIX>The early . . . It had to be the late forties.

420
00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:39.176
<v SHELTON>And the early fifties.

421
00:24:39.176 --> 00:24:39.471
<v FELIX>Fifties.

422
00:24:39.471 --> 00:24:41.529
Like I said, sewage just got here.

423
00:24:41.529 --> 00:24:44.765
<v CRAMER>You said dirt . . . There were dirt roads before you got streets?

424
00:24:44.765 --> 00:24:45.353
<v FELIX>Oh yes.

425
00:24:45.353 --> 00:24:45.647
<v SHELTON>Yes.

426
00:24:45.647 --> 00:24:49.471
<v FELIX>We didn't . . . This is the only paved road, the OST [Old Spanish Trail].

427
00:24:49.471 --> 00:24:52.706
Our road was dirt roads . . . I mean, it was dirt roads.

428
00:24:52.706 --> 00:24:57.118
You get bogged . . . It would rain, it would get muddy, and all of that stuff.

429
00:24:57.118 --> 00:24:57.706
<v SHELTON>Oh yeah.

430
00:24:57.706 --> 00:25:01.824
<v FELIX>We didn't see that until . . . I'd say maybe the late forties and early fifties.

431
00:25:01.824 --> 00:25:03.000
<v SHELTON>Had to be.

432
00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:11.506
<v FELIX>Had to be the late forties . . . because we came [?] in the forties and it was not done . . . Because I remember we missed the [?] . . . So,

433
00:25:11.506 --> 00:25:13.987
it had to be the late forties.

434
00:25:13.987 --> 00:25:15.759
<v CRAMER>So, you mention a community.

435
00:25:15.759 --> 00:25:18.241
You mentioned Papa . . . You mentioned Ham Reed.

436
00:25:18.241 --> 00:25:25.329
Who were some of the community leaders at the time, when you were growing up . . . in the forties and fifties?

437
00:25:25.329 --> 00:25:29.582
<v FELIX>Okay now, we were . . . We've always been a parish, and a [?] community.

438
00:25:29.582 --> 00:25:29.937
<v SHELTON>[sneezes]

439
00:25:29.937 --> 00:25:30.646
<v CRAMER>Bless you.

440
00:25:30.646 --> 00:25:33.127
<v FELIX>Ham Reed was one of those politicians.

441
00:25:33.127 --> 00:25:34.899
He was from Lake Charles.

442
00:25:34.899 --> 00:25:37.734
He was . . . Ham Reed, he was a sheriff.

443
00:25:37.734 --> 00:25:39.152
<v SHELTON>He was a sheriff.

444
00:25:39.152 --> 00:25:39.861
<v FELIX>Sheriff, yeah.

445
00:25:39.861 --> 00:25:41.987
He was a sheriff out here.

446
00:25:41.987 --> 00:25:48.367
But in order to be nice . . . to get votes, he was nice to [laughs] the community members here.

447
00:25:48.367 --> 00:25:55.456
Therefore, you know, he kind of catered to you in a sense . . . to help you get what you really needed.

448
00:25:55.456 --> 00:25:56.873
That's who that was.

449
00:25:56.873 --> 00:25:58.291
Papa was my grandfather

450
00:25:58.291 --> 00:25:59.000
<v SHELTON>Yes.

451
00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:00.290
<v FELIX>That was a strong man.

452
00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:03.903
He was . . . He had a lot of wits and a lot of common sense.

453
00:26:03.903 --> 00:26:05.194
<v CRAMER>Now, which grandfather was he?

454
00:26:05.194 --> 00:26:05.710
<v FELIX>My mom's.

455
00:26:05.710 --> 00:26:07.000
<v CRAMER>So his name was . . . ?

456
00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.000
<v FELIX>Frank Fischer.

457
00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:08.500
<v CRAMER>Okay.

458
00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:09.500
<v FELIX>Frank Fischer.

459
00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:12.000
<v CRAMER>AKA [also known as] Papa?

460
00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:14.000
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[talking excitedly, indistinguishable]

461
00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:18.954
<v FELIX>He used to . . . I used to like to watch him working with his wild horses.

462
00:26:18.954 --> 00:26:23.578
When a horse is born, they're wild . . . So, therefore you had to tame them.

463
00:26:23.578 --> 00:26:25.560
He did a lot of that.

464
00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:27.211
We used to ride horses.

465
00:26:27.211 --> 00:26:32.165
I can remember when we rode a horse one time . . . me and my sister Barbara.

466
00:26:32.165 --> 00:26:34.147
We [rode that horse through?] Maplewood.

467
00:26:34.147 --> 00:26:37.119
That horse saw a . . . They used to say "see

468
00:26:37.119 --> 00:26:37.780
<c.p>a ghost."

469
00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:45.706
That horse saw a ghost and ran away and knocked us off that horse! [laughs] I didn't want to ride a horse ever since.

470
00:26:45.706 --> 00:26:50.000
My arm was in a sling for I don't know how long.

471
00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:52.857
<v CRAMER>You said you had horses then, growing up?

472
00:26:52.857 --> 00:26:53.929
<v FELIX>He had horses.

473
00:26:53.929 --> 00:26:55.000
He had cows.

474
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:02.857
Yeah, we grew around all of that kind of stuff. . . Because, what he didn't have. . . He had the horses and the cows.

475
00:27:02.857 --> 00:27:06.429
We also had guineas and turkeys and stuff like that.

476
00:27:06.429 --> 00:27:13.214
Then, on the highway, my grandparents had the pork, the guineas again, and all that stuff . . . the sugarcane syrup.

477
00:27:13.214 --> 00:27:16.429
They made syrup and all that kind of stuff.

478
00:27:16.429 --> 00:27:20.357
So, one family had this and the other family had that.

479
00:27:20.357 --> 00:27:22.143
That's the way it was.

480
00:27:22.143 --> 00:27:25.000
<v CRAMER>So you grew cane to make the syrup?

481
00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:25.714
<v FELIX>Oh, yeah.

482
00:27:25.714 --> 00:27:26.071
Yes.

483
00:27:26.071 --> 00:27:28.929
<v SHELTON>We did that . . . My mother taught me how.

484
00:27:28.929 --> 00:27:32.143
<v FELIX>He had a whole field behind the house, yes.

485
00:27:32.143 --> 00:27:32.500
<v CRAMER>Wow.

486
00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:40.357
There's so much that we want to come back and talk with you about. . . But, to kind of start wrapping things up,

487
00:27:40.357 --> 00:27:50.000
one of the things I want to ask you is . . . What places were some of the most important places in Mossville when you were growing up?

488
00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:51.039
<v FELIX>The school's one.

489
00:27:51.039 --> 00:27:52.771
<v SHELTON>School was the most important.

490
00:27:52.771 --> 00:27:55.542
<v FELIX>Yes, because that's where you met your friends.

491
00:27:55.542 --> 00:27:57.275
You were with your friends.

492
00:27:57.275 --> 00:27:59.007
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>[discussing school and friendship, indistinguishable]

493
00:27:59.007 --> 00:28:01.778
<v FELIX>School was one of the most important things.

494
00:28:01.778 --> 00:28:04.895
Then, the church was a good place to be.

495
00:28:04.895 --> 00:28:08.359
I was Methodist, and my father was a Holiness Sanctified.

496
00:28:08.359 --> 00:28:13.556
She . . . my grandmother, which is my dad's mom, used to cook and cook for everybody.

497
00:28:13.556 --> 00:28:20.484
They would have picnics on the grounds . . . Because sometimes they'd go to church and stay at church all day long.

498
00:28:20.484 --> 00:28:25.680
Therefore, they would put that food on that wagon and bring it to the church.

499
00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:31.915
I could leave my church because I was Methodist, and I would go down there and eat. [laughs]

500
00:28:31.915 --> 00:28:33.993
<v SHELTON>We had about three churches then.

501
00:28:33.993 --> 00:28:34.686
<v FELIX>Three, yes!

502
00:28:34.686 --> 00:28:35.725
See we had. . .

503
00:28:35.725 --> 00:28:38.843
<v SHELTON>Mt. Zion, The Holiness Church, and the Methodist church.

504
00:28:38.843 --> 00:28:41.961
The Methodist church was there on the corner of . . .

505
00:28:41.961 --> 00:28:43.000
<v FELIX AND SHELTON>Prater Road.

506
00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:44.586
<v SHELTON>And Old Spanish Trail.

507
00:28:44.586 --> 00:28:47.362
Mt. Zion was there at the end. . .

508
00:28:47.362 --> 00:28:48.155
<v FELIX>Right there.

509
00:28:48.155 --> 00:28:49.741
It must've been a [?].

510
00:28:49.741 --> 00:28:52.517
You had other churches way down there.

511
00:28:52.517 --> 00:28:56.086
<v SHELTON>Across the track. [Felix and Shelton discussing churches, indistinguishable]

512
00:28:56.086 --> 00:28:58.466
<v FELIX>On this end, on this side.

513
00:28:58.466 --> 00:29:03.621
That had to be the . . . Sanctified Church [Felix and Shelton discussing churches, indistinguishable]

514
00:29:03.621 --> 00:29:06.000
<v CRAMER>Which church is still standing?

515
00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:06.725
<v FELIX>Neither one.

516
00:29:06.725 --> 00:29:08.538
The Mt. Zion is remodeled.

517
00:29:08.538 --> 00:29:09.625
It's still there.

518
00:29:09.625 --> 00:29:13.250
The original church is not there, but it's still there.

519
00:29:13.250 --> 00:29:14.338
That's the oldest.

520
00:29:14.338 --> 00:29:17.962
I think they celebrated a hundred and forty eight years . . .

521
00:29:17.962 --> 00:29:19.050
<v SHELTON>I believe so.

522
00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:20.138
<v FELIX>Not long ago.

523
00:29:20.138 --> 00:29:22.312
My church . . . Hurricane Rita destroyed it.

524
00:29:22.312 --> 00:29:26.662
We did not rebuild because it was going to cost too much.

525
00:29:26.662 --> 00:29:29.562
They just tore that one down last June.

526
00:29:29.562 --> 00:29:30.288
<v SHELTON>Yeah, yeah.

527
00:29:30.288 --> 00:29:31.375
<v FELIX>Was that right?

528
00:29:31.375 --> 00:29:31.738
<v SHELTON>Yes.

529
00:29:31.738 --> 00:29:32.825
<v FELIX>The Morning Star.

530
00:29:32.825 --> 00:29:35.000
<v SHELTON>Morning Star Christ Sanctified Church.

531
00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:36.038
<v CRAMER>What other places?

532
00:29:36.038 --> 00:29:40.192
Where there any other places where, you know, the young folks gathered?

533
00:29:40.192 --> 00:29:41.577
<v SHELTON>Another entertainment they had, [?]

534
00:29:41.577 --> 00:29:42.615
<v FELIX>Yeah, oh yeah.

535
00:29:42.615 --> 00:29:44.000
<v CRAMER>What was that?

536
00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:45.702
<v SHELTON>It was a club.

537
00:29:45.702 --> 00:29:49.957
But for children, for us, we had Ms. Lula's place.

538
00:29:49.957 --> 00:29:51.234
We called it . . .

539
00:29:51.234 --> 00:29:52.085
<v CRAMER>What's that?

540
00:29:52.085 --> 00:29:54.213
<v SHELTON>We called it the canteen.

541
00:29:54.213 --> 00:29:56.340
We'd go there on Sundays.

542
00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.745
That's where we'd dance and [. . . ?] meet our friends.

543
00:29:59.745 --> 00:30:00.170
<v CRAMER>Yeah?

544
00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:04.851
<v SHELTON>At that time, they had . . . built us a swimming pool across. . .

545
00:30:04.851 --> 00:30:05.702
<v FELIX>Over there.

546
00:30:05.702 --> 00:30:06.979
<v SHELTON>From the school.

547
00:30:06.979 --> 00:30:10.383
We had the swimming pool and the canteen.

548
00:30:10.383 --> 00:30:15.064
That was all our entertainment other than church and school. [laughs]

549
00:30:15.064 --> 00:30:19.745
<v CRAMER>So, music-wise . . . Just curiously, what kind of music would be played?

550
00:30:19.745 --> 00:30:24.000
<v FELIX>We were kids who would get along and play together.

551
00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:28.681
Didn't have to get into too much trouble, got along well.

552
00:30:28.681 --> 00:30:39.319
She has a brother that was growing up when we were growing up, and he used to be our preaching when we'd have church. [laughs]

553
00:30:39.319 --> 00:30:41.021
<v SHELTON>He's a preacher now!

554
00:30:41.021 --> 00:30:48.255
<v FELIX>He's a real preacher now! [laughs] [Felix, Shelton, and Cramer laugh and discuss, speaking over one another,

555
00:30:48.255 --> 00:30:55.915
indistinguishable] [. . . ?] Coke bottles with a rope hanging out of them! [everyone laughing more] We had a good time.

556
00:30:55.915 --> 00:30:56.766
<v SHELTON>Oh, yeah.

557
00:30:56.766 --> 00:31:03.149
<v FELIX>We will never, never say that we hate those moments that we had to live.

558
00:31:03.149 --> 00:31:04.000
<v SHELTON>No.

559
00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:05.086
<v FELIX>It was precious.

560
00:31:05.086 --> 00:31:06.895
It was . . . I always say,

561
00:31:06.895 --> 00:31:14.493
it's the best little town in the world to be in . . . because we do not have to worry about so crime.

562
00:31:14.493 --> 00:31:22.092
You have crime everywhere, but the kind of crime that we have here . . . If somebody did something to some family here,

563
00:31:22.092 --> 00:31:26.434
you can always track it down and find out who did it.

564
00:31:26.434 --> 00:31:28.967
You can go right to the source.

565
00:31:28.967 --> 00:31:30.053
<v SHELTON>Talk to them.

566
00:31:30.053 --> 00:31:31.500
<v FELIX>. . . and go to it.

567
00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:35.842
It's not like some stranger coming here and being here . . . Yeah, no,

568
00:31:35.842 --> 00:31:39.822
no. That's why I continue to say . . . When I leave here,

569
00:31:39.822 --> 00:31:44.888
I want to be able to compare the place I relocate to a Mossville.

570
00:31:44.888 --> 00:31:48.868
That's why I'm not so hasty in going to just anything.

571
00:31:48.868 --> 00:31:55.020
I want to be able to live in a community that I can almost compare to Mossville.

572
00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:58.276
It won't be Mossville, but it would be almost.

573
00:31:58.276 --> 00:31:59.000
<v SHELTON>Yeah.

574
00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:13.167
<v CRAMER>So what's the one thing that is not out there in history books . . . and that hasn't been said by anybody yet . . . that you would like people to know about Mossville?

575
00:32:13.167 --> 00:32:14.583
<v FELIX>[pauses] Mossville was . . .

576
00:32:14.583 --> 00:32:19.778
<v SHELTON>Best little close-knit town that you ever want to be in.

577
00:32:19.778 --> 00:32:24.500
<v FELIX>[. . . ?] Mossville was a safe haven for African-Americans to live in.

578
00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:30.639
It might have been one of the largest African-American sections in this area.

579
00:32:30.639 --> 00:32:39.611
So, therefore, it was a very safe place for African-Americans to be, because it was one hundred percent African-Americans.

580
00:32:39.611 --> 00:32:50.000
It was located far enough from the hostility, that they had nothing to worry about . . . Just live like a big family.

581
00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:56.567
<v SHELTON>You know this town produced [recorder feedback and rustling papers] a lot of . . . Well,

582
00:32:56.567 --> 00:33:01.258
educated people and entrepreneurs . . . I even had that written down.

583
00:33:01.258 --> 00:33:10.170
Like I have down here . . . A well-travelled community activist, Dorothy Hartman . . .[Felix and Shelton laugh] was a Mossville graduate.

584
00:33:10.170 --> 00:33:11.108
Oh, yeah!

585
00:33:11.108 --> 00:33:12.985
So many other things.

586
00:33:12.985 --> 00:33:19.552
We produced a lot of firsts . . . firsts here and [papers loudly rustling] firsts there.

587
00:33:19.552 --> 00:33:26.588
It produced a lot of successful people, entrepreneurs . . . operators, plant operators, plant supervisors, accountants, engineers.

588
00:33:26.588 --> 00:33:32.216
A lot of them came from right here. . . Mossville, the Mossville High.

589
00:33:32.216 --> 00:33:35.969
<v FELIX>Teachers that we had! [laughs] Those caring teachers.

590
00:33:35.969 --> 00:33:40.191
<v SHELTON>Yes, yes. [Felix interjects something, laughs, indistinguishable] Nurses, doctors . . .

591
00:33:40.191 --> 00:33:41.129
<v FELIX>Yes, yes.

592
00:33:41.129 --> 00:33:41.598
<v SHELTON>Doctor [?].

593
00:33:41.598 --> 00:33:42.536
A priest.

594
00:33:42.536 --> 00:33:49.103
<v FELIX>Like I say, I wouldn't trade this for no other community in the world.

595
00:33:49.103 --> 00:33:55.670
<v CRAMER>Thank y'all so much, both of you, for sharing your stories with us today.

596
00:33:55.670 --> 00:34:02.237
On behalf of the center, and on behalf of the museum and everybody here,

597
00:34:02.237 --> 00:34:08.335
I just can't express enough gratitude to you for participating in this project.

598
00:34:08.335 --> 00:34:21.000
With that, I'd like to close this interview, but I'll let y'all think about doing interview number two and finishing up at some point in time.

599
00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:27.844
<v FELIX>You know, I think it's going to take a long time for this to be finished.

600
00:34:27.844 --> 00:34:35.544
There is so much that can be said and that needs to be said and should be said.

601
00:34:35.544 --> 00:34:38.967
As she said . . . I've travelled quite a bit.

602
00:34:38.967 --> 00:34:41.106
Even though I hate travelling,

603
00:34:41.106 --> 00:34:52.228
I've travelled a lot just to be able to make sure that our community gets . . . or is noticed . . . and get recognition for a lot of things.

604
00:34:52.228 --> 00:34:59.928
I was even invited to . . . Barbara [Batson?] invited me to one of the hearings there in Washington D.C.

605
00:34:59.928 --> 00:35:04.206
It's things like that that I want to have here.

606
00:35:04.206 --> 00:35:08.911
One of the other main things that . . . There's an organization, MEAN.

607
00:35:08.911 --> 00:35:12.761
The organization I'm with . . . It's Mossville Environmental Action Now.

608
00:35:12.761 --> 00:35:16.183
Mossville . . . Mean . . . together with Monique Hartman, Natalie Walker,

609
00:35:16.183 --> 00:35:24.311
Wilma Subra . . . were able to file the first ever, in the United States, human rights petition in Washington DC.

610
00:35:24.311 --> 00:35:31.156
That was because they had put that together so well that it could not be denied.

611
00:35:31.156 --> 00:35:36.717
That is one of the main things that I want everybody to know.

612
00:35:36.717 --> 00:35:38.000
<v SHELTON>To know.

613
00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:43.165
<v FELIX>It is the only one ever filed in the United States.

614
00:35:43.165 --> 00:35:44.104
I have . . .

615
00:35:44.104 --> 00:35:45.512
<v CRAMER>That is very . . .

616
00:35:45.512 --> 00:35:47.860
<v SHEARMAN>Could you repeat that please?

617
00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:48.799
<v FELIX>Oh! [laughs]

618
00:35:48.799 --> 00:35:51.146
<v CRAMER>We've got it on tape.

619
00:35:51.146 --> 00:35:53.494
<v FELIX>I have it right here!

620
00:35:53.494 --> 00:36:05.701
I brought it so you could see I'm telling you the truth. [all laugh, audio feedback] Mossville Environmental Action Now, which is also known as MEAN,

621
00:36:05.701 --> 00:36:16.500
along with Monique Hartman, Wilma Subra, Natalie Walker . . . In March of 2005 filed a first ever human rights petition in the United States.

622
00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:21.195
It had never been one filed before we did that.

623
00:36:21.195 --> 00:36:26.829
It was filed with the . . . Let's see . . . Organization of American . . . Washington D.C.

624
00:36:26.829 --> 00:36:31.994
We physically went over and handed those documents to that organization.

625
00:36:31.994 --> 00:36:35.750
That was in March of . . . March 7, 2005.

626
00:36:35.750 --> 00:36:49.366
<v CRAMER>Alright, well we're going to go ahead and close this up so we can burn these CDS and get them to you . . . and get you to sign the paperwork.

627
00:36:49.366 --> 00:36:53.122
Again, thank you ladies so very, very much.

628
00:36:53.122 --> 00:36:55.000
<v FELIX>I have the . . .

629
00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:55.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4404.

630
00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:55.200
End Session I.]
